[00:00.00]Hello, everyone. Today, we're going to dive into some important ways to structure sentences in English, specifically negative and interrogative forms. Are you ready? Let's go. First, let's look at the negative sentences. Notice how these sentences include the word don't or doesn't. This is how we say that something is not happening. For example, we don't write letters anymore means that letter writing isn't something we do now. These kinds of sentences help us express what we don't have, don't do, or don't like. Now let's switch gears to interrogative sentences. Interrogative is just a fancy word for questions. Look at questions like, what do you like to read? Or who is your favorite author? These examples show how we use question words like what, when, and who to find out more about someone's preferences or habits. Notice how questions often start with these words, followed by a helper verb like do or does. You might see that on the right side, there's the same information in Portuguese. This is super handy if you're learning both languages. It helps to see how we ask questions and make negative statements in both English and Portuguese. I encourage you to try making your own sentences, both negative and interrogative. Practice is the best way to master these concepts and make your English even better. Great job today, everyone. Keep practicing and you'll see amazing progress.